</div>

<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>An about page has one job in its first sentence</h2>
		<p>Answer "who is this and why should I keep reading". Everything else — the history, the degree, the philosophy — is the second read, and most people never take it.</p>
		<p>So the order is fixed: <strong>a face</strong>, <strong>one sentence that makes a claim</strong>, <strong>numbers that back it</strong>, <strong>a way to get in touch</strong>, and only then the prose. The numbers row is the part most about pages are missing, and it is the cheapest credibility on the page.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Built from</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>The head</td><td><code>im-abouthero</code> — a face beside the claim, stacking under 40rem.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The numbers</td><td><code>im-expstats</code>, the same ruled row a <a href="/collections/project/page/">project</a> and a <a href="/collections/travel/trip/">trip</a> use.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The rail</td><td>Now, <a href="/collections/project/">hire me</a>, elsewhere, the letter. Not an author card — this page <em>is</em> the author card.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>The work</td><td><a href="/collections/project/card/">Project cards</a>, three of them, with their CTAs.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h2>Write "I will tell you not to do it" somewhere</h2>
		<p>Not that sentence specifically — the thing it stands for. An about page made entirely of claims about how good somebody is reads as an advertisement. One paragraph that costs the author something is what makes the rest of it believable.</p>
	</div>
</section>
